                    <a href="/blogs/ryanc/archive/2007/03/28/wpf-bootcamp-day-3-hardware-capability.aspx">Wednesday, March 28, 2007 4:05 PM</a>                
                    by
					<a href="/members/cromwellryan/default.aspx">cromwellryan</a> 
                </div>

               <p>To avoid disappointing users after they&#39;ve gotten as far as running your application on their 1997 300mhz Pentium II, determine their capabilities using the <a href="http://msdn2.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.windows.media.rendercapability.tier.aspx">RenderCapability.Tier</a> value before hand and notifying them that they&#39;ll need to joing the 21st century before running your app.&nbsp; </p> <p><a title="RenderCapabilities.Tier" href="http://blogs.msdn.com/henryh/archive/2006/08/23/719568.aspx">Henry Hahn</a> has a quick overview and, the&nbsp;always welcome, code samples of using this value to make your application conform to the different tiers.</p> <p>Don&#39;t forget your bit shifting, cause the Tier is set up to support new Tiers/platforms in future releases.</p><div style="clear:both;"></div>

